  "final_text": "**Don't enter 279.7 MWh yet. That figure covers only 11 months, because the September 2025 invoice is missing.**\n\nWhat I checked in `electricity_2025.csv`:\n\n- **The duplicate was removed correctly.** October (EL-25-10, 38,700 kWh) appears twice, once as an e-invoice and once as a scan, with the same invoice number and amount. Counting it once is right.\n- **September 2025 has no row at all.** The months run 2025-01 to 2025-08 and then jump to 2025-10.\n- **279.7 MWh is the sum of those 11 months:** 279,700 kWh with October counted once. The note's arithmetic is right, but it isn't a full year.\n\nThe gap matters. Your company profile says tank cooling peaks in September and October, and October alone was 38.7 MWh. So the missing month is probably one of the largest of the year. Judging by August (32.1) and October (38.7), September was likely around 30–40 MWh. That would put the full year at roughly **310–320 MWh**, about 10–14% above the draft figure.\n\n**What to do:**\n1. Get the September 2025 invoice (EL-25-09) from Lumivolt Snabduvanje or from your accounting records. It may simply not have been exported into the CSV.\n2. Full-year figure = 279.7 MWh + September's kWh ÷ 1,000. Enter that.\n3. If the bank needs a number before the invoice arrives, tell them it's an estimate and say how you got it (for example, the average of August and October, about 35.4 MWh, giving about 315 MWh). Don't present an estimate as metered data, especially for a green credit line where the baseline may be checked against invoices.\n\nYou could also update the office manager's note: it currently says \"full year\" and \"can go to the bank.\"",
